406
01:18:30.190 --> 01:18:50.700
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): completed that task, and here are the side effects of the task, or I've rejected the task, or, you know, there's a next step you need to do. Very, very simple. There's 350 trust tasks defined already. They're extremely strong. One of the things in the Decentralized trust group is they have a risk and harm prevention

407
01:18:50.700 --> 01:18:55.399
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Kind of research team, and doing systematic reviews.

408
01:18:55.510 --> 01:19:07.049
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): it's been focused very much on these trust tasks and looking at, you know, how they work. You then take those trust tasks and create trust ceremonies out of them. So, trust ceremony is…

409
01:19:08.390 --> 01:19:29.169
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): if I need to stitch together more than one task to do something, like joining a community, or I need to do some KYC, I might need to check a few other things, that is a ceremony, we'll talk about that. Credentials, consent. And then at the top of this are the applications. OpenVTC, which is the CLI tool that Linux kernel developers will use.

410
01:19:29.170 --> 01:19:32.340
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): there's, agent memory, MCP bridges.

411
01:19:32.340 --> 01:19:40.170
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): AI tooling, whatever it is that you want. And this kind of loosely follows the trust over IP model as we go through it.

412
01:19:41.120 --> 01:19:46.190
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): And we kind of talked through that. So, one of the big…

413
01:19:46.480 --> 01:19:53.930
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): things we did was there was a workshop in January, February, some of you around the table and online were there.

414
01:19:54.160 --> 01:20:00.080
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): And we realized that while there's a lot of standards and specifications.

415
01:20:00.980 --> 01:20:20.619
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): there was very little that was working across it, and people who had been trying to build across the standards, you just run straight away into incompatibility issues. And so we decided that what we needed to do was build the code at the same time the specifications are being drafted and worked on.

416
01:20:20.640 --> 01:20:21.680
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): And…

417
01:20:22.160 --> 01:20:31.130
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): In hindsight, yes, it was the right thing to do, but at the time, it was a little controversial. People were not fully convinced on it.

418
01:20:31.130 --> 01:20:44.789
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): But I would say that what has happened from those as we build, is it's not that the build is ahead of the specs, it's not that the specs are ahead of the build. They're kind of doing this all the time and moving around, but what I love is that we're getting evidence

419
01:20:45.230 --> 01:20:54.159
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): of… Where the specs are really solid and absolutely helping clarify things, but also where…

420
01:20:54.380 --> 01:21:14.169
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): they just simply don't. I'm not going to go into the details, but if you're on any of the working groups, you'll see there are issues galore being talked about, PRs happening left, right, center, and that's good. That's what you want to see in a vibrant community. Some of those communities were pretty dead before this occurring. They'd kind of been frozen in time.

421
01:21:14.170 --> 01:21:16.919
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): As you go through this. So…

422
01:21:17.810 --> 01:21:23.360
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Let's see, have a look here. This is kind of some of the examples of what you can do.

423
01:21:23.500 --> 01:21:25.749
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Based on this, so…

424
01:21:26.360 --> 01:21:34.709
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): one of the concepts of your VTA is the VTA holds your keys, and it holds your credentials, and can hold, kind of, metadata.

425
01:21:35.020 --> 01:21:38.249
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): when that VTA is working on your behalf.

426
01:21:38.400 --> 01:21:50.709
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): it's not handing out the keys. You can slightly treat it like a signing oracle. You can kind of hand in information, and it will generate the signatures for you, hand them back, it can enroll.

427
01:21:50.710 --> 01:22:00.620
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): and revoke devices that you connect to the VTA, and therefore to other aspects. There's a lot of really fun things in there that you can do with the VTA.

428
01:22:00.980 --> 01:22:14.259
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): the… what it actually holds is a whole bunch of information. So your VTA is holding all of your key material, and signing keys, and you have control whether they leave the VTA,

429
01:22:14.770 --> 01:22:16.020
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Or…

430
01:22:16.970 --> 01:22:38.840
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): as in they don't leave the VTA, or they're actually going to be given to certain applications and services as a proxy as you go through this, and I'll show you how that works with OpenVTC when we get a demo. It's a credential store, so it does all the things, issues, holds, presents, and revokes your credentials, everything you expect there. It's a secrets vault, so again, we want to kind of shift left.

431
01:22:38.930 --> 01:22:50.990
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): and work with existing websites, so it can be a password manager for you if you want. It has agent memory, so you can actually tie your AI agent to your VTA, VTA,

432
01:22:50.990 --> 01:22:59.130
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): and use that as a durable, secure store for your AI interactions, which is really, really powerful.

433
01:22:59.320 --> 01:23:05.270
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): when you take that capability with a VTC, you can create AI community memory.

434
01:23:05.910 --> 01:23:09.049
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Which is really powerful, so this is one of the things that we want to unlock.

435
01:23:09.230 --> 01:23:13.920
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): whether it's before Linux Plummer's event or afterwards, but imagine

436
01:23:13.920 --> 01:23:36.779
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): the DTG working group had an AI shared community memory that had all the specs, latest decisions being made, everything in one place, so that when you join that, and you say, I'm going to use an AI agent to help just distill and explain things, that AI agent, instead of trying to figure it out on its own, is now talking to the DTG shared core memory as you go through this.

437
01:23:36.780 --> 01:23:40.330
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): A lot of people ask, how do we move so fast?

438
01:23:40.340 --> 01:24:05.070
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): on the build, if you're kind of looking at the build site, that's part of the secret right there, is everything is going into my VTA memory for myself, and my agents work off it. It has application state. So one of the fun things, if you've ever, configured an application, then you lose the device, and you think, oh crap, I didn't back up the application, I've lost that aspect, the VTA can do that for you. It has approval policies, so

439
01:24:05.070 --> 01:24:14.910
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): So, a big thing you'll see on the VTA and VTC is everything is policy-driven. And when we say policy-driven, it's open policy agents, OPA.

440
01:24:14.910 --> 01:24:23.339
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): And one of the things we've done is taken the policy management and pushed that into the approval chains. So, for the first time.

441
01:24:23.620 --> 01:24:35.730
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): you can have intelligence as to, depending on who is asking for an approval, and what the trust task they need approval for, I can handle that approval flow differently.

442
01:24:35.990 --> 01:24:41.050
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): So, again, this comes from the expectation that in the future.

443
01:24:41.650 --> 01:24:48.240
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): you don't want to wake up in the morning in an AI world and say, good morning, Glenn, you have 123 things to approve.

444
01:24:48.850 --> 01:25:08.319
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Right? That is what is going to happen. It's going to be in GDPR all over again, approve all cookies. Just click, click, click, click, click, right? So, what I can do is I can say, actually, my VTA, you are allowed to approve some things that are low risk on my behalf. I may plug in an AI agent to deal with things that are of medium risk.

445
01:25:08.700 --> 01:25:13.829
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): And then it's only certain critical things that meet the policy criteria I set.

446
01:25:14.150 --> 01:25:23.689
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): that comes to me asking for a human intervention. The idea being, if I only get asked once a day, once every two days, I'm going to pay a lot more attention to it.

447
01:25:23.800 --> 01:25:36.320
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): than the 100-plus approvals a day, if we're going to do this. And again, this is really powerful. This also works, by the way, in the VTC. So, a really good example here are things like a family unit.

448
01:25:36.380 --> 01:25:51.469
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): As a parent, do you want to approve things on behalf of your children? You can start programming that, for example. Do you want to approve things on behalf of elderly parents that might be at risk of getting scammed? You see an unusual financial transaction. You can program

449
01:25:51.470 --> 01:25:59.180
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): The approval to be, hey, all of the kids, the adult children, if 3 out of the 4 approve it, then it's okay.

450
01:25:59.370 --> 01:26:10.879
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): So you can really do a lot of fun things with this. Consent management, device agents, identity and operations with this as you go through. So, lots and lots of things you can do with it.

451
01:26:10.880 --> 01:26:29.890
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): One of the questions that comes up a lot is, well, what about in the VTA? Do I just have one identity? No, you do not. You have, trust contexts. Trust context, you can break up however you want. This is just one example. I can have my personal IDs for Apple, Google, Facebook. Each of those is a context.

452
01:26:29.890 --> 01:26:34.179
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): What that means, it's like a logical partition. They can't see each other.

453
01:26:35.190 --> 01:26:42.709
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): And I can start doing policy, saying anything impersonal, always keep separate from work. Apple can't see Google.

454
01:26:42.970 --> 01:26:47.829
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): something at the personal level can see Apple, Google, Facebook. You can really do…

455
01:26:47.990 --> 01:26:59.479
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): whatever you want there. This makes it really easy, by the way, if you sign up to, like, a new site or a new application, you just create a context, and if 24 hours later you don't like it, just blow the context away.

456
01:26:59.480 --> 01:27:08.130
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): everything gets shredded with that trust context when it goes away, as you go through it. This also means, by the way, you can have multiple VTAs.

457
01:27:08.440 --> 01:27:25.169
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): So you don't have to have one VTA doing everything. You can have a mesh of VTAs, and there's actually a fleet management tool that helps you manage that, and so you can start spreading out the trust context across a fleet of VTAs, if you want.

458
01:27:25.520 --> 01:27:44.399
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): just be warned, it gets very, very complex very, very, very quickly, as Jeff and I will attest on some of this, and you'll see a bit of a multi-VTA demo as you go through it. The verifiable Trust Community. This one's really simple. A community owns its own policies. We will have out-of-the-box policies that allows a community

459
01:27:44.400 --> 01:27:48.119
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): To get started, but they get to choose what it means to join.

460
01:27:48.120 --> 01:27:52.569
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Must have proof you're an adult, must be in Switzerland, must be a member of DIF.

461
01:27:52.670 --> 01:27:59.670
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Sure. Whatever it is that you want, you get to set. That is set at a policy level, then there's a…

462
01:28:00.330 --> 01:28:20.570
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): policy engine that runs all that for you. As you go through the trust ceremony, there are side effects created, and those side effects are things such as you got accepted, and here is proof of that by a verifiable membership credential, a verifiable endorsement credential. The VRCs, the relationship credentials you create within that community.

463
01:28:21.840 --> 01:28:36.950
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): And people outside that community, if they want to ask, you know, Glenn, are you really working on the Linux kernel project, and if so, are you a contributor, or are you maintainer, or are you a core level? Well, that's where the trust registries come in. You can ask the trust registries.

464
01:28:36.950 --> 01:28:51.110
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): that every VTC has for that type of information, and they get to choose what goes in those trust registries, who can talk to the trust registries, whether they're truly public, or you must have a credential saying you're recognized by the trust network.

465
01:28:51.120 --> 01:29:10.999
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): So a lot of this, is all here. All of this runs on DIDs. If you… I just find it amazing that even in our own industry, people still don't understand, really, what a DID is. So, again, they really do three things, and you must understand these three things are the entire backbone everything in VTI works on.

466
01:29:11.000 --> 01:29:13.690
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): First of all, it is the unique identifier.

467
01:29:13.920 --> 01:29:19.700
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): They are globally unique. Second, they are what holds your public key infrastructure.

468
01:29:20.200 --> 01:29:26.929
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Proof of ownership is sign something with the key in the DID. If you can't sign it, you don't have ownership of the DID.

469
01:29:27.090 --> 01:29:35.390
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): As simple as that. The third one is, what are the service endpoints? How do you reach me? How do you talk to me?

470
01:29:36.610 --> 01:29:46.400
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Anna, do I talk to you using DITCOM, or REST, or TSP? Do you have a trust registry? Are you a community? Are you an individual? All of those

471
01:29:46.530 --> 01:30:03.090
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): details are the service endpoints in your DID document, as you go through it. The ones we support by default, you can kind of see there the usual suspects. I will say for us, Did WebVH is unashamedly the star of the show.

472
01:30:03.090 --> 01:30:23.529
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): It's designed to be, you know, highly, highly durable. It's verifiable history, because, and you'll see this, there are DIDs already that we are using in this stack that have got hundreds of changes to them as the keys are getting rolled, service endpoints change, etc, and WebVH just works

473
01:30:23.590 --> 01:30:41.360
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Beautifully, chef's kiss, and it's extremely performant compared to other DID methods that try to do this, as you go through it. So I think, Stephen, you're on the call, thank you. Great protocol, as you go through it. Things are cached.

474
01:30:41.740 --> 01:30:59.319
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): with a DID. It's okay. You can have this kind of immutable methods, like DID key. If you want to change the key, it's an entire new DID with a new identifier. Or it can be mutable, like WebVH, and others that you go out there. I will say that,

475
01:31:00.010 --> 01:31:03.770
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): if you want to work with a VTI stack, there's kind of a…

476
01:31:03.950 --> 01:31:08.990
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): few core protocols you must support. If you can talk, did WebVH?

477
01:31:09.130 --> 01:31:20.860
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): you use Curve25519 as your cryptography algorithms, and you can talk DIDCOM or TSP. You can fully interact with everything in the VTI stack.

478
01:31:21.510 --> 01:31:26.209
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): That's the basic 3 things you need. If you don't have those 3 things.

479
01:31:26.500 --> 01:31:39.459
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): It's just another walled garden, and you can't, talk to it. Everything else is built on those fundamentals, as you go through. In terms of then, how transport works, so…

480
01:31:39.720 --> 01:31:43.370
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): there's your one VTA for simplicity.

481
01:31:44.000 --> 01:32:02.430
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): There's the trust tasks, which really kind of define how this works, and I'll send some links out for this. But how you transport the information across, we actually don't care. It can be Trust Spanning Protocol, it can be DIDCOM v2, it can be REST,

482
01:32:02.430 --> 01:32:22.610
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): There's libptp coming. These are just different transport mechanisms that work, and this is being used right now. When you see the demos, you'll see DIBCOM, TSP, and REST all being used interchangeably. The system does not care. We do have a weighted matrix, which is, if you have a DID document that says, I talk REST,

483
01:32:22.740 --> 01:32:28.300
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): DIDCOM, and TSP. It will negotiate in order of TSP first.

484
01:32:28.730 --> 01:32:42.480
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): If that doesn't work, fall back to DIDCOM. If DIDCOM doesn't work, fall back to rest. And that's, again, based on the opinion, from a security and performance perspective, we think they go in that order, in terms of just modern aspects and encryption.

485
01:32:44.050 --> 01:32:56.260
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): one of the important things with any messaging system is, well, how do I talk to you if you are on an air-gapped network, if you're behind NAT gateways, if you're moving around,

486
01:32:56.550 --> 01:33:11.800
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): The great thing about your VTA, if you don't use REST, which we would not recommend for a VTA, then you can move your VTA anywhere in the world, and there's no URL that you have to keep updating and repointing via DNS to where it goes.

487
01:33:11.950 --> 01:33:25.969
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): you've got these messaging relays, or mediators that allow you to abstract where you are, and this is what allows you to punch holes through firewalls, through NAT gateways. It can obfuscate where you exactly are. There's some

488
01:33:26.230 --> 01:33:28.600
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Pretty clever things you can do.

489
01:33:28.810 --> 01:33:52.740
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): again, I come from a bit of an interception background, so you can do things like hide behind mediators in different countries to create constant timing attacks, or even based on latency, I don't know where you are. And you can do some really fun routing, such as mediator to mediator to mediator. So, for example, if Darrell and I want to send messages to each other, but I might have Jeff in the middle.

490
01:33:54.010 --> 01:33:59.099
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Darrell really never knows what my mediator I'm using. Jeff will know what mediator I'm using.

491
01:33:59.100 --> 01:34:22.770
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): and Jeff will pass that to Darrell, but I won't know, actually, what mediator might be between Jeff and Darrell as you go through this. So this is where you get a whole bunch of security coming in. If you've ever built an async messaging bus that has multi-hop routing like this, read between the lines, it's super complex, hard to get working. I wouldn't say we've got it perfect yet, but it's going really well. So what does it look like on the wire?

492
01:34:22.770 --> 01:34:29.590
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): It's pretty efficient. One of the things, again, you'll see this in the demo.

493
01:34:29.630 --> 01:34:34.710
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): You may be thinking, problem with anything decentralized, it's fat, heavy, and slow.

494
01:34:35.950 --> 01:34:43.820
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): You're not wrong, but what we've been doing is driving this to an efficiency where, for example, the authentication flow we have

495
01:34:44.350 --> 01:34:48.719
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): is the fastest authentication flow on the internet right now.

496
01:34:49.520 --> 01:34:55.340
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): It's faster than anything else. Anything OpenID, OAuth, even simple username and password.

497
01:34:55.510 --> 01:35:19.030
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): it's as fast or faster. It's crazy what it does, and it's because of, some of the things it's doing. Basically, at the inner is your trust task document. It is also signed. You then wrap that in the messaging layer, sealed to the recipient. There's then routing envelopes that go around it. Again, we don't care whether it's lib P2P, DIDCOM TSP, and then you have, transport agnostic.

498
01:35:19.030 --> 01:35:27.040
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Whether that goes over a WebSocket, HTTPS, HTTP3QUIC, you can print it on a piece of paper, for all I care.

499
01:35:27.040 --> 01:35:31.840
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): it doesn't matter. And again, this is really important, and, you know, Adam.

500
01:35:31.950 --> 01:35:37.139
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): our CTO, you're saying is, you know, if you're going to do it, do it two or more types, because it just…

501
01:35:37.140 --> 01:35:53.040
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): makes you have an interface and abstraction point for it, and so this is some of that design thinking coming into this. What this means from an implementation point of view, if you use the Trust Task Library, which is all CodeGen, so you get that for free.

502
01:35:54.460 --> 01:36:02.020
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): It also has the bindings, which means you can plug and play, and move from DIDCOM to TSP,

503
01:36:02.240 --> 01:36:11.690
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): without really any code changes whatsoever. And this is something that, before we had this library, it was almost impossible to do, from people as you go through.

504
01:36:12.340 --> 01:36:29.780
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): in terms of what this looks like in reality, and why we are big fans of TSP, I was a big fan of DidCom. I love DIDCOM. Still has a soft spot for… in my heart. But the problem that DidCom has is, as you start to route it.

505
01:36:29.940 --> 01:36:35.470
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): If you do lazy routing, You're losing some of your privacy.

506
01:36:36.010 --> 01:36:53.419
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): I can see everyone in between Drummond and I can see that Glenn and Drummond are trying to send a message to each other. So the way you would protect that is I would nest the envelopes. I would… so I'm going to send a message to Drummond, but I'm going to send it via Adam, Adam's going to send it via Anna, and every time you do that, the message grows

507
01:36:53.590 --> 01:37:06.750
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): 1.6 to 1.8 times per hop. Or a, you know, kind of 100 kilobyte message quickly gets 20 to 40 megabytes in just 10 hops, which is not that many out there.

508
01:37:09.140 --> 01:37:13.380
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): So what TSP does is TSP…

509
01:37:13.680 --> 01:37:32.930
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): brings a lot of what DIDCOM does, but that 10 hops of 100 kilobytes only grows to about 120 to 130 kilobytes. Which, again, if you're dealing with mobile devices, you know, I live in Southeast Asia, networks are not always the best in developing countries, people on cheap devices.

510
01:37:32.930 --> 01:37:37.350
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): You don't want to have, very heavy protocols, or be sending around

511
01:37:37.530 --> 01:37:43.090
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): megabytes of traffic as you go through. So, this has been really, really good learning.

512
01:37:43.430 --> 01:37:51.090
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): when we started this, we didn't know that we would be using TSP until we ran straight into the DIDCON size library. Yep.

513
01:37:51.230 --> 01:38:00.890
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): What about post-quantum computing crypto primitives? Yeah, so fully, fully supported, so… This will bloat all your,

514
01:38:00.980 --> 01:38:14.790
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Not, not by too much, actually. Okay. Not by too much. So if you look at, like, MLDSA44, the keys get bigger, but the actual message being transferred over to the glass, it's still the same size. Okay, cool. Yeah.

515
01:38:15.170 --> 01:38:27.750
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): The speed at encrypting and decrypting slows down a bit. But it's not too bad. Again, depends which PQC you're choosing to use, but…

516
01:38:28.230 --> 01:38:30.810
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Yeah, all right.

517
01:38:32.330 --> 01:38:40.060
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Trust tasks, I'm not going to go through this in detail, but basically everything is a URI, 350 specs, 5 transport bindings.

518
01:38:40.160 --> 01:38:56.580
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): Basically, what happens is, you have the ceremonies, and you can effectively build whatever you want out of them. Everything in VTI that you'll see is all running over a trust task of some form, and the ceremonies that go through this.

519
01:38:57.030 --> 01:39:05.069
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): You can also do some fun things, and I'll give you a demo of this in a second, is…

520
01:39:05.390 --> 01:39:13.419
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): One of the problems let's talk about DID management. If I have a WebVH DID,

521
01:39:13.420 --> 01:39:28.790
Drummond Reed (First Person Cooperative): It has pre-rotation key set up, for example, which means you can only legitimately rotate to the next log entry if you have proof that you can have ownership of the pre-rotation key, and you sign the next log entry with one of those keys.

